  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe need to find a step function h: [0,2] -> [0,1] that minimizes the maximum overlap integral, which is the worst-case correlation between h and its shifted versions. The current best-known bound is 0.3808, and the parent solution achieves 0.380861. \n\nOur approach is a **multi-resolution hybrid strategy** that combines several powerful optimization techniques:\n- **Sparse representation**: We view the step function as a sparse interaction pattern between key intervals, reducing dimensionality while preserving structure.\n- **Resolution adaptation**: We optimize at multiple resolutions to escape local minima and gradually refine the solution.\n- **Gradient-guided perturbations**: We use analytical gradients for efficient search in continuous space.\n- **Binary embedding**: We periodically decode continuous states into binary configurations to validate global minima.\n- **Block-coordinate descent**: We update function segments independently to balance exploration and exploitation.\n\nThe central idea is to leverage the parent solution as a warm start while systematically probing lower-dimensional representations and binary variants to break through the current barrier. This is justified because:\n- Sparse representations can uncover underlying structure missed by dense optimization.\n- Multi-resolution search escapes local optima by exploiting problem scaling.\n- Binary embeddings validate if discontinuous structures could yield better overlap bounds.\n- Gradient-based methods provide efficient refinement around promising seeds.\n\nThe main limitations are:\n- We assume the Erd\u0151s problem requires discontinuous structures.\n- Strict binary states may not optimally interact with continuous perturbations.\n- The approach is heuristic but leverages both local and global search strategies.\n\nThis plan is distinct from previous solutions in its core representation shift (sparse vs dense) and hybrid nature without predefined block constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- **Representation**: \n  - Primary: Continuous step function h(x) defined on [0,2]\n  - Secondary: Binary representation \u03c7(x) \u2208 {0,1} with exactly N/2 measure\n  - Tertiary: Piecewise linear interpolation between sparse points\n\n- **Objective**: Minimize max<sub>k\u2208[0,2N-1]</sub> (dx \u00d7 cross-correlation at lag k)\n\n- **Constraints**:\n  - 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n  - \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\n- **Key variables**:\n  - Let S be a set of K sparse intervals with lengths {l\u1d62} and values {v\u1d62}\n  - Binary embedding: N/2 points uniformly distributed with specific value combinations\n\n- **Objective equivalence**:\n  max<sub>k</sub> \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = \n  max<sub>k</sub> [ (total_measure_of_h - \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)h(x+k)dx) ]\n  Since total_measure_of_h=1.\n\n- **Binary embedding**:\n  We ensure all states satisfy {0,1} where \u222b\u2080\u00b2 \u03c7(x)dx = N/2 when using N-point samples.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nInitialization:\n- Start with provided parent solution (n=500 samples) if available\n- Project parent into [0,1] and satisfy integral constraint (sum(h) \u2248 n/2 with clipping)\n\nCore optimization phases:\n\nPhase 1: Sparse-mode optimization (k=5-10 intervals)\n- Sample K intervals uniformly across [0,2]\n- Assign K-dimensional vector v where each component corresponds to interval value \n- Initial embedding: values = {0.2, 0.3, ..., 0.9} \n- Project embedding to satisfy integral constraint (correlated with total length using linear interpolation)\n- Optimize over K and v using block-coordinate gradient descent (analytical gradient from: \n  \u2202/\u2202v\u1d62(max\u2096\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx) = \n  -sum\u2096 (area where h(x)=1 and (x+k)\u2208interval\u1d62) * 2 * dx)\n\nPhase 2: Sparse-to-dense refinement\n- For current sparse solution, compute dense interpolation with linear segments (depth=4)\n- Project interpolated function to [0,1] and adjust integral by redistributing mass\n- Evaluate correlation layer by layer from coarser to finer grids\n\nPhase 3: Binary embedding and validation\n- Convert current solution to binary mode by thresholding (at v=0.5)\n- Project binary state to satisfying integral constraint (exactly N/2 points set to 1)\n- Re-evaluate correlation to check for global minima in discontinuous space\n- If lower bound found, use that configuration as new base state\n\nPhase 4: Adaptive hybrid search\n- Maintain 3 working sets: current, finest, and archive\n- At each iteration, probabilistically choose:\n  - Update one sparsity parameter (K) by 10%\n  - Update one interval value by \u00b15%\n  - Randomly perturb one-dimensional segments \n- Use line search guided by negative gradients (Armijo rule)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n- **Initial parameters**:\n  - Basic configuration: K=10 intervals, n=400 samples\n  - Computational budget: 60% for sparse mode, 20% for refinement, 20% for binary validation\n\n- **Scheduled adaptations**:\n  - Every 10 iterations: Increase K by 2 if correlation plateau\n  - Every 20 iterations: Double resolution (n\u00d72) if current C\u2085 above 0.38\n  - Every 30 iterations: Activate binary embedding if total_time > 300s\n\n- **Perturbation rules**:\n  - Center of mass move: Shift a sparse interval by \u00b10.1 while balancing integral\n  - Block value mutation: Randomize value of a contiguous block by quantile shift\n  - Gaussian field jitter: Add local noise to function value\n\n- **Stopping conditions**:\n  - Time budget exceeded: Captures best solution found\n  - No improvement in 200 iterations: Switch to lowest resolution version\n  - Binary validation confirms 0.382 bound: Terminate early\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n- Domino effect: Resolutions n=250, 500, 1000 with dx scaling handled\n- Progressive computation: Start sparse-first then add resolution depth\n- Recovery: Use finest level solution as fallback if phase fails\n- Built-in parallelism: Wait 30s for expensive operation before timing out\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- **Feasibility checks**:\n  1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  2. np.abs(sum(h) - n/2) < 1e-3\n  3. np.all(np.isfinite(c_5_output))\n\n- **Objective consistency**:\n  recompute = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * (2.0 / n_points)\n  ensure abs(our_c5 - recompute) < 1e-6\n\n- **Termination checks**:\n  - Without parent: Use binary configuration check against theoretical bounds\n  - During refinement: Return immediately if C\u2085 < 0.3808 and no room for improvement\n\nBest solution is returned from the finest valid representation. Any intermediate solution is discarded if higher resolution improves it.",
